# Line endings, which decide whether a clone runs at all.
#
# This project is developed on Windows, so without these rules every shell
# script would be committed with CRLF and a Linux clone would fail on the very
# first line:
#
#     ./setup.sh: /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# That is not a cosmetic problem - it is the whole Linux path broken by an
# invisible character. So the rule is set here rather than left to whatever
# core.autocrlf each machine happens to have.

# Sensible default: normalise text in the repository, check it out natively.
* text=auto

# Scripts are run by an interpreter that reads the shebang literally, so these
# must be LF everywhere, including in a Windows checkout.
*.sh   text eol=lf

# And these are read by cmd.exe, which wants CRLF.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Binaries git must never touch.
*.gguf binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.f32  binary
*.db   binary
